# features-delta-one-service Development Rules

This is features-delta-one-service: computes technical indicators, momentum, volume, and related features (~4,596 features across ~20 groups) for spot and futures.

## Codex References

This service follows unified-trading-codex principles:
- 01-domain/[batch|live]/per-service/features-delta-one-service.md
- 04-architecture/batch-live-symmetry.md
- 04-architecture/concurrency.md (MAX_WORKERS for CPU-bound)
- 02-data/schema-governance.md (output_schemas.py, validate_timestamp_date_alignment)
- 03-observability/lifecycle-events.md (3-tier event logging)
- 06-coding-standards/ (quality gates, testing, config)

## Workspace Rules

Inherits all rules from workspace `.cursorrules`: quality gates, quickmerge, config standards, cloud-agnostic design, event logging, UTC datetime, imports at top.

- Config: extend UnifiedCloudServicesConfig; NEVER os.getenv() or hardcoded project IDs
- Error handling: use @handle_api_errors, @handle_storage_errors; NEVER bare except

## Quality Gates

See: .cursor/rules/quality-gates/quality-gates.mdc. Same ruff==0.15.0 across Local/GitHub Actions/Cloud Build. UV only. Python 3.13.

## Key Paths

- Entry: `features_delta_one_service/__main__.py`, `cli/main.py`
- Calculators: `features_service/app/calculators/` (technical.py, momentum.py, volume_flow.py, etc.)
- Schemas: `features_delta_one_service/schemas/output_schemas.py`

## Service-Specific Patterns

- **Calculator plugin pattern**: Feature groups live in `features_service/app/calculators/` (technical.py, momentum.py, volume_flow.py, etc.); extend base calculator
- **Live mode embedding**: In live mode, embeds market-data-processing + market-tick-data-handler (no separate pipeline)
- **validate_timestamp_date_alignment()** before every GCS write (schema governance)
- **MAX_WORKERS: 1** — CPU-bound due to TA-Lib; parallelization risks thread-safety issues

## Domain Rules

- ALWAYS validate instrument_id format and ensure no lookahead bias in feature calculations
- NEVER add features that use future data; use lookback windows only
- Batch: reads from market-data-processing + features-calendar outputs; Live: consumes ticks directly via embedded handlers
- Features must be deterministic and reproducible for the same input date range
- Feature groups: technical (RSI, MACD, etc.), momentum, volume_flow, microstructure, oscillators, volatility, returns, targets, streaks, market_structure

## Dependencies

- **Upstream**: market-data-processing, features-calendar
- **Downstream**: ml-training (required), ml-inference, strategy
- See: unified-trading-deployment-v2/configs/dependencies.yaml

## Context7

When working with TA-Lib, pandas, or NumPy, append "use context7" to prompts for up-to-date APIs and best practices.

## CLI / Modes

- `--mode batch`: reads from GCS (market-data-processing, features-calendar outputs)
- `--mode live`: embeds market-data-processing + market-tick-data-handler; consumes ticks directly
- Batch-live symmetry: same engine, different data source and sink

## Testing

See: .cursor/rules/testing/test-quality-standards.mdc. Unit tests for calculators (lookback, NaN, lookahead bias). tests/unit/test_event_logging.py required. Synthetic fixtures; no real GCS. Regression: test_regression_{issue}_{description}.

## Codex Maintenance

When establishing new calculator patterns or feature groups, update unified-trading-codex and this file.
See .cursor/rules/codex-maintenance.mdc for workflow.

## Universal Reminders

- datetime: ALWAYS `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`; NEVER datetime.now() or datetime.utcnow()
- Storage: use `get_storage_client()` from unified_cloud_services; NEVER `from google.cloud import storage`

See: .cursor/rules/core/always-use-quickmerge.mdc for quickmerge workflow.

## Anti-Patterns

- NEVER use MAX_WORKERS > 1 (TA-Lib is not thread-safe)
- NEVER hardcode GCS paths; use `config` and cloud-agnostic `get_storage_client()`
- NEVER skip `validate_timestamp_date_alignment()` before writes
- NEVER add imports inside functions; imports at top of file only
- NEVER use print(); use logger.info() / log_event()
